Social Death defined as a situation or circumstance in which a person is completely shunned by society, being unable to participate in certain social events such as employment.

Social Death can occur when people commit a societally unacceptable sin or error that makes associating with said person untenable. This can arise in the form of a crime, allegations of a crime (meaning rumors that are not
founded in legal action), largely inappropriate comments (most notably in the form of racial comments), or other frowned upon acts. In an increasingly large scale economy, how do those who are deemed unfit for society get 
by if not by underground or black market means?

Dig into implications and possible examples of Social Death, most literature will probably be tied to those who get left behind following terms of incarceration. Might just lead to divide between US and European prison 
models. Then analyze outcomes; if capital punishment is not pursued for extreme cases and rehabilitation is not attempted/executed well enough, what happens? Might lead to topic of capital punishment, which will probably not
be further examined. More of a moral/philosophical issue than economic. May be interested in further research on rehabilitation and possible differences between the United States (which still has cases of capital punishment)
and the EU (which has banned capital punishment). Due to the lack of capital punishment, there is further emphasis on rehabilitation. Might be useful to research crime statistics in the EU, if information is available. 

A good example would be someone working in an industry for a number of years and a scandalous situation (let's say that information came out that 20 years ago an offensive term was used by said person) makes the person 
completely unemployable. They lose their job and are unable to get another comparable one due to the situation. 
I think another good example would be a police officer who is involved in a scandal involving a shooting. Say the shooting was completely unjustified and they lose their job, but they are not imprisoned. They spent decades 
working in said position,all of their work experience is in being a police officer. Even in the face of the shooting being perhaps potentially justified, someone with such a record is perhaps not someone who should continue 
in that line of work. But denying them that line of work would completely cut them off.
